1.5 Pull Back the Curtain: Print What You Sent
You still cannot see what left your machine, so print it. This script builds the request as data and shows it to you before anything is sent. It runs without a model, without a network, and it prints the same thing every time.
# Listing 1.2 of 4: the request, as data (payload.py)
import json

model = "llama3"
messages = [
    {"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant."},
    {"role": "user", "content": "In one sentence, what does a post office do?"},
]
payload = {"model": model, "messages": messages}

print(json.dumps(payload, indent=2))
{
  "model": "llama3",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "system",
      "content": "You are a helpful assistant."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "In one sentence, what does a post office do?"
    }
  ]
}
This is the letter from the analogy, written as JSON. JSON is a text format for structured data, built from names, values, lists in square brackets, and groups in curly braces. You can read this one already. At the top level there are two names. model says which model should answer. messages holds a list of two messages, and each message is a group with a role and a content.
The user message is yours. It is the question you typed in Listing 1.1, unchanged. The system message above it is the interesting one. You did not write it. get_response put it there, because its default instruction to the model is the string “You are a helpful assistant.” Every call in Listing 1.1 quietly sent two messages, and the first one was an instruction about how to behave. Chapter 2 is entirely about that first message, and about how much of a chatbot’s apparent personality is one string in a list.
The list has room to grow. A conversation is this same list with more messages in it: your turns and the model’s replies, in order, resent in full on every call. Chapter 3 shows why products built on this call go to that trouble.
